s.** It piped `cargo test` into `tee` and keyed off the *pipeline's* exit status (i.e. `tee`'s). When the temp path was unwritable, `tee` failed and failing tests were reported as passing. Now keys off `cargo test`'s own exit code, plus a floor on the test count.\n\n2. **`cargo test` silently tested a stale `.so`.** With `crate-type = [\"cdylib\"]`, `cargo test` never builds or refreshes the `cdylib`, so tests `dlopen`ed whatever artifact was on disk. My harness originally rebuilt only when the file was *missing* — not enough. It now rebuilds unconditionally, verified both ways (poisoned-but-present artifact gets refreshed; fresh source bug is caught with no manual build).\n\n## Caveat worth recording\n\n`c_src` builds with `C_FLAGS = -fPIC` and no `-O` flag, i.e. **`-O0`**. `div_euclid(INT_MIN, -1)` executes `INT_MAX + 1`, which is **undefined behaviour** in C; at `-O0` GCC emits a plain `addl` that wraps to `INT_MIN`, which the Rust `wrapping_add` matches. That equality is a property of *this build*, not of the C standard — at higher optimization levels GCC could exploit the UB and diverge. I treated the C-as-built as ground truth and changed nothing.\n\nI also audited that **no input can crash the C** (every `/` and `%` provably has a non-zero divisor and non-`INT_MIN` dividend on its path, so the x86 `idiv` `SIGFPE` is unreachable) — that's what made exhaustive sweeping safe.\n\n**Files added** (`c_src/` untouched apart from the required `build/` output; `src/lib.rs` unchanged; only `libloading` added to `[dev-dependencies]`): `SYMBOLS.md`, `ERRORS.md`, `CONFIGS.md`, `VERIFICATION.md`, `run_all.sh`, `mutate.py`, and `tests/{common/mod.rs, phase_b_configs.rs, phase_c_errors.rs, phase_d_symbols.rs}`."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":553,"cache_read_input_tokens":132320,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":553,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier