 is **undefined behaviour** in C; at `-O0` GCC emits a plain `addl` that wraps to `INT_MIN`, which the Rust `wrapping_add` matches. That equality is a property of *this build*, not of the C standard — at higher optimization levels GCC could exploit the UB and diverge. I treated the C-as-built as ground truth and changed nothing.\n\nI also audited that **no input can crash the C** (every `/` and `%` provably has a non-zero divisor and non-`INT_MIN` dividend on its path, so the x86 `idiv` `SIGFPE` is unreachable) — that's what made exhaustive sweeping safe.\n\n**Files added** (`c_src/` untouched apart from the required `build/` output; `src/lib.rs` unchanged; only `libloading` added to `[dev-dependencies]`): `SYMBOLS.md`, `ERRORS.md`, `CONFIGS.md`, `VERIFICATION.md`, `run_all.sh`, `mutate.py`, and `tests/{common/mod.rs, phase_b_configs.rs, phase_c_errors.rs, phase_d_symbols.rs}`.","ttft_ms":3283,"ttft_stream_ms":3118,"time_to_request_ms":38,"type":"result","duration_ms":1058169,"uuid":"f823a8de-7555-4b75-bb6b-afc08ea676d6","queued_turn_count":0}
